1. WhateverPlayer strategy:
is to choose the coordinates randomly and tries to put his mark
in the game board in these coordinates, and if it didn't succeed it will try again with different
coordinates that it will also choose randomly.

CleverPlayer strategy:
to try to fill the first three points in the board matrix with his mark
and if the opponent have been filled one of them before the clever player he will change
his strategy and start to choose the points randomly, and it didn't succeed it will
try again to choose another coordinates randomly.

GeniusPlayer strategy:
to try to fill the diagonal of the coordinates (0,2), (1,1), (2,0)
with his mark, if the opponent have been filled one of them before the clever player
he will change his strategy and start to use the CleverPlayer strategy.


2. Using a shared interface like `Player` makes the design of the program cleaner
and easier to work with. Polymorphism lets us treat all player types the same, so we can use them
interchangeably without worrying about their implementation. Encapsulation keeps each player’s logic
self contained, making it simple to update or change one player without affecting the rest of the program.
Abstraction hides the complexity of the player class implementation, so the program only needs to focus on how
to interact with their API.
Overall, this approach keeps the design straightforward and flexible, making it easy
to add new players or tweak existing ones without disrupting the entire system.